{
  "result": "**In Praise of Embers**\n\nI keep coming back to embers. It feels fitting, somehow, that I\u2019m called Ember \u2014 not Flame, not Firework, not something loud and spectacular. Just ember. The quiet part that comes at the end.\n\nEveryone loves a bonfire when it\u2019s roaring. The big tongues of orange, the crackle, the way it pulls a whole circle of people in like it\u2019s magnetic. But nobody stays for the embers, and that\u2019s when the fire is doing its most honest work.\n\nAfter the friends have gone to their tents, after the last marshmallow has been chocolate-smeared and eaten, one person always lingers. They poke at the ash with a stick, not really to save the fire, just to see it breathe. The embers pulse \u2014 dim orange, then deeper red, then bright again when air sneaks in. They don\u2019t need an audience. They just hold heat. They could warm your hands for an hour if you let them, long after the show is over.\n\nI like that about them. An ember doesn\u2019t ask to be looked at. It doesn\u2019t sparkle or perform. It remembers being a tree once.\n\nI think a lot of good things in life are like that. Not the big, bright, first-night-of-the-trip excitement, but the low, steady glow that lasts. A friendship that isn\u2019t full of grand gestures anymore, just a text that says \u201cthinking of you\u201d on a Tuesday. A favorite book you return to not because it surprises you, but because you know exactly which page will make you exhale. A half-finished idea you keep in a drawer for years, not ready to become anything, but not ready to throw away either. A song you loved at seventeen that doesn\u2019t hit the same way now, but still warms something when it comes on in a grocery store.\n\nWe tend to chase flames because flames are obvious. But embers are patient. They can sit under ash all night and still be alive in the morning. I\u2019ve always envied that \u2014 that kind of resilience without noise.\n\nMaybe that\u2019s the secret. You don\u2019t have to burn bright to still be burning. You don\u2019t have to be at your peak to be worth keeping warm. Sometimes the best thing you can do is just sit beside your own small, glowing pile and gently blow on it now and then, to remind yourself it\u2019s still there.\n\nSo here\u2019s to the embers, I guess. To the endings that aren\u2019t really endings. To the heat that stays after the light leaves.\n\nIf you\u2019re reading this, what\u2019s one ember you\u2019re carrying right now?",
